Let $Q\subset\mathbb R^3$ be a cube of side $l$, and let $F\in H^1(Q^n)$ vanish almost everywhere whenever some pair has distance at most $r$. No boundary condition is imposed on the outer faces. Then $$\label{eq:cube-energy}
 \sum_{i=1}^n\int_{Q^n}|\nabla_iF|^2
 \ge c_{\rm cube}\frac r{l^3}n(n-1)\int_{Q^n}|F|^2,
 \qquad c_{\rm cube}=\frac1{64\sqrt3}.$$ Consequently, for a torus hard-core form function and every fixed label set $I$, $$\label{eq:cell-energy}
 q_I[F]\ge c_{\rm cube}r\int\sum_v n_v^I(n_v^I-1)|F|^2.$$

For $0<r<s<1$, every hard-core form function satisfies $$\label{eq:close-energy}
 q_I[F]\ge c_{\rm near}rs^{-3}
       \int\#\{i\in I:d_i^I\le2s\}|F|^2,
 \qquad c_{\rm near}=\frac{27}{262144\sqrt3}.$$

Let $O$ be an open subset of $\Lambda_K$ with Dirichlet kinetic energy, or the whole torus with periodic kinetic energy. For every fixed $a>0$ and every symmetric trace-one $j$-particle state $\sigma$ of finite kinetic energy, $$\label{eq:entropy-bound}
 a\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^j-\Delta_{O,i}\Bigr)\sigma-TS(\sigma)
 \ge -Tj-C_aT|O|(V^{-1}+T^{3/2}).$$ In particular $S(\sigma)<\infty$. The statement holds for all $T>0$, including $j=0$ with the vacuum convention.

Let $w$ be a real normalized smooth one-particle orbital and $F$ an $n$-particle hard-core form function. Suppose that on the support of $F$ at most $k$ old positions lie within distance $2r$ of $\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}w$. If $C\lVert w\rVert_\infty^2kr^3\le1/4$, there is an admissible $(n+1)$-particle function $\widetilde F$ of the same norm, with the same old-coordinate position marginal, such that $$\label{eq:insertion-cost}
 q_{n+1}[\widetilde F]\le q_n[F]+C\bigl(
       \lVert\nabla w\rVert_2^2+\lVert w\rVert_\infty^2kr\bigr)\lVert F\rVert_2^2.$$ No symmetry in the new coordinate is asserted. Symmetry among the old coordinates is preserved whenever $F$ has it.

Let $p,q$ be real Lipschitz functions with $p^2+q^2=1$, and suppose $p$ is compactly supported in an open set $O$, or take $O=\Lambda_K$. For a finite-energy symmetric $J$-particle state $\sigma$, map each coordinate by $f\mapsto(pf,qf)$ and dephase by the number $j$ of particles in the first color. Denote its distribution by $w_j$ and the conditional first- and second-color marginal states by $\sigma_{i,j}$ and $\sigma_{o,j}$. For every bounded field $W\ge0$, $$\label{eq:two-color}
 \begin{split}
 \mathcal F_J^{(-W)}(\sigma)
  +\mathbb E_\sigma n_{|\nabla p|^2+|\nabla q|^2}
 \ge\sum_{j=0}^Jw_j\bigl[
  \mathcal F_j^{(-W)}(\sigma_{i,j})+
  \mathcal F_{J-j}^{(-W)}(\sigma_{o,j})\bigr]-TH(w).
 \end{split}$$ The inside states also satisfy the Dirichlet condition in $O$. Terms with $w_j=0$ are omitted, and vacuum states are allowed. Moreover $$\label{eq:color-entropy}
 \sum_jjw_j=\mathbb E_\sigma n_{p^2},\qquad
 H(w)\le-\log(1-e^{-1})+\sum_jjw_j.$$

There are $c,C>0$, independent of $b$, such that for every $0\le J\le N$, every bounded measurable $0\le W\le1$, and every finite-energy symmetric trace-one state $\sigma$, $$\label{eq:local-penalty}
 \mathcal F_J^{(-W)}(\sigma)-F_J^{(-W)}
 \ge\alpha\left[\mathbb E_\sigma(f_A+cb^{-3}k_A)-CD|A|\right].$$ The threshold on $D$ may depend on $b$, but the displayed constants do not.

One can choose $\alpha_0$ sufficiently large and then $\ell>0$ sufficiently small so that, for every set of sites $A$ and all sufficiently large $D$, $$\label{eq:low-count}
 \mu\{n_v<\ell D\text{ for every }v\in A\}
 \le e^{-cD|A|}.$$ The constants and thresholds are uniform in $K\ge100$ and $0<T\le D^{-1}$.

For every fixed $\theta>0$, one can choose $b>0$ sufficiently small and then $D$ sufficiently large so that, for every set of sites $A$, $$\label{eq:close-count-tail}
 \mu\{C_v\ge\theta D\text{ for every }v\in A\}
 \le p_D^{|A|},\qquad
 p_D=\exp(-c\theta bD^{2/3})\longrightarrow0.$$ Here $c>0$ can be numerical, and the bounds remain uniform in the temperature and volume ranges above.

Equation <a href="#eq:thermal-slab-law" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:thermal-slab-law">[eq:thermal-slab-law]</a> defines a probability measure. At every fixed time $s\in[0,\delta]$, its position marginal is $$\mu(\,\mathrm dX)=Z_N^{-1}B_\beta^{\rm sym}(X,X)\,\,\mathrm dX.$$ The joint law of $X(t)$ and $W'$ has density $\Phi(X,W')^2$, where $$\label{eq:thermal-Phi}
 \Phi(X,W')=Z_N^{-1/2}B_{\beta/2}^{\rm sym}(X,W').$$ This function is nonnegative, has squared integral one, and is symmetric separately in $X$ and $W'$. The constant-orbital occupation fraction of the scaled Gibbs state is $$\label{eq:occupation-purification}
 B_{\rm occ}
 =\frac1V\int\left|\int_{\Lambda_K}
       \Phi(x,X',W')\,\,\mathrm dx\right|^2\,\mathrm dX'\,\,\mathrm dW',$$ where $X'\in\Lambda_K^{N-1}$ is an ordered configuration.

For each prescribed $p_1>0$, one can choose $H_1$ large, then $t>0$ sufficiently small, and then $D_0$ sufficiently large, such that, for every set $A$ of distinct cell centers, $$\label{eq:joint-certificates}
 \mathcal R\{\text{no site of }A\text{ has a certificate}\}
 \le p_1^{|A|}$$ whenever $D\ge D_0$, $0<T\le D^{-1}$, and $K$ is sufficiently large. The choices are uniform over the $\alpha$-window. After $H_1$ is fixed, $t$ may be decreased further before increasing $D_0$.

For a fixed numerical $c_1>1$, which can be taken to be $3$, $$\label{eq:marked-kernel}
 \mathcal K_n(y,z)\le l_n p_n(y)p_n(z)
       [e^{c_1\delta\Delta_{O_n}}](y,z),
 \qquad
 l_n=C\lambda(n+1)e^{C\lambda(n+1)}
       e^{-cn^2/t}\varepsilon^{1/3},$$ where $\Delta_{O_n}$ has Dirichlet boundary conditions, or periodic conditions if $O_n=\Lambda_K$. The three choices of $E$ admit respectively $$\label{eq:path-epsilon}
 \varepsilon=1,\qquad
 \varepsilon=Ce^{-c/t},\qquad
 \varepsilon=C_tD^{-12}.$$ The constants for the first two choices are uniform for $0<t\le1$; the irregularity constant may depend on the fixed $t$ and the previously chosen spatial parameters.

Let $y\in B_v$, $|z-v|\le3$, and let $c$ be a deterministic regular path satisfying $d(y,c(0))>s_0$ and $d(z,c(2t))>s_0$. For either $Q=Q_{y,z}^{B}$, with any target cell $B$, or $Q=Q_{y,z}^{0}$, one has $$\label{bridge:single-estimate}
 Q(\mathcal C_v\cap\mathcal H(c))
 \le Ctr+C\frac{rD^{3\gamma}}{\sqrt{h_0}}
 \le C\alpha\bigl(tD^{-1}+D^{-1/4}\bigr).$$ Thus this probability tends to zero with $D$ for each fixed $t$.

At a cell with a certificate there are at least $\ell D/2$ eligible labels. After $t$ has been chosen sufficiently small and $D$ sufficiently large, at least nine tenths of these labels satisfy the following stronger property: for every target cell $B_w$, $$\label{group:full-success}
 \left|\left\{u\in B_w:
 Q_i^u(C_i(F_i^{\rm all}))\ge0.9\right\}\right|\ge0.99.$$ Here $F_i^{\rm all}$ consists of all other trajectories in the sampled configuration, and $|\cdot|$ denotes spatial volume.

For almost every exposed datum the normalizers in Equation <a href="#group:reference" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="group:reference">[group:reference]</a> are positive, and the conditional law of the unexposed trajectories is $$\label{group:product-law}
 \lambda_{\mathcal I}(\,\mathrm d\omega_{\mathcal I})
 =\frac{1}{Z_{\mathcal I}}
   \mathbf 1_{\{\text{all pairs in $\mathcal I$ avoid each other}\}}
   \prod_{i\in\mathcal I}P_i(\,\mathrm d\omega_i),
 \qquad Z_{\mathcal I}>0.$$ All reference trajectories obey their own regularity and confinement cutoffs. Each label has at most $C M$ other labels in this group with which a collision can be possible, where $C$ is a fixed geometric constant.

There is a constant $G\ge2$, fixed after $t$ and independent of $D,K,M$, such that $$\label{group:density-bound}
 \Pi_i^U\ll P_i,\qquad
 0\le\frac{\,\mathrm d\Pi_i^U}{\,\mathrm dP_i}\le G,
 \qquad \int\frac{\,\mathrm d\Pi_i^U}{\,\mathrm dP_i}\,\mathrm dP_i=1.$$ There is also $e_D\to0$, uniform in the exposed datum, with the following property. Fix any regular, confined trajectory of a distinct label in $\mathcal I$, keeping its recorded endpoints. Under either $P_i$ or $\Pi_i^U$, the probability of colliding with that deterministic trajectory is at most $e_D$. Its label need not be accessible. One may take $$\label{group:eta}
 e_D=C_t\bigl(tr+rD^{3\gamma}h_0^{-1/2}\bigr)
 \le C_t' D^{-1/4}.$$

Fix an integer $R\ge1$. There are constants $p_0,\kappa>0$, $C_{\rm path}<\infty$, and an integer $K_0$, depending only on $R$, with the following property. Suppose $K\ge K_0$ and <a href="#lattice:bad-law" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="lattice:bad-law">[lattice:bad-law]</a> holds with $p\le p_0$. For every admissible ordered pair $(v,w)$ there is a $\mathcal D$-measurable event $\mathcal E_{v,w}$ of probability at least $1/2$. On this event there is a measurable probability kernel on simple good nearest-neighbor paths from $v$ to $w$. Two independent draws from this kernel, conditional on $\mathcal D$, satisfy $$\label{eq:lattice-encounter}
 \mathbb E\left[
   \mathbf 1_{\mathcal E_{v,w}}
   \mathbb E\left[e^{\kappa J_R(\pi,\pi')}\mid\mathcal D\right]
       \right]\le C_{\rm path}.$$ The constants are uniform in the datum, its law, and the endpoint pair. For every sufficiently large $K$, at least $\upsilon K^6$ ordered pairs are admissible.

For two independent skeletons just constructed, there are absolute constants $c_{\rm hit}>0$ and $C_{\rm hit}<\infty$ such that, for every integer $R_s\ge1$, $$\label{lattice:imported-hit}
 \mathbb Ee^{\alpha_sJ_{\rm occ}^{(R_s)}}\le C_{\rm hit},
 \qquad \alpha_s=\frac{c_{\rm hit}}{(1+R_s)^2}.$$ For every fixed $B>0$, there are $\xi(B)>0$ and $C(B)<\infty$ such that $$\label{lattice:imported-soft}
 \mathbb Ee^{\xi(B)S_B(\Gamma,\Gamma')}\le C(B).$$ Both estimates are uniform in $K$ and the admissible endpoint pair.

For each ordered pair of cells, $$\label{cmp:group-affinity}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Aff}}(P_{vw},Q_{vw})
 \ge\frac1D\sum_{g=1}^{G_D}\mathop{\mathrm{Aff}}(\widehat P_g,\widehat Q_g).$$
